      "text": "Given $x - \\frac{1}{x} = 4$, we can square both sides to get:\n\\[\n\\left(x - \\frac{1}{x}\\right)^2 = 4^2,\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[\nx^2 - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 16,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nx^2 - 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 16.\n\\]\nAdding 4 to both sides gives us:\n\\[\nx^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 18.\n\\]\n\nNext, we square $x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2}$ to find $x^4 + \\frac{1}{x^4}$:\n\\[\n\\left(x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2}\\right)^2 = 18^2,\n\\]\nwhich is:\n\\[\nx^4 + 2 \\cdot x^2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{1}{x^4} = 324,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nx^4 + 2 + \\frac{1}{x^4} = 324.\n\\]\nSubtracting 2 from both sides gives:\n\\[\nx^4 + \\frac{1}{x^4} = 322.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find $x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3}$. We use the identity:\n\\[\n\\left(x - \\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\left(x^2 + 1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}\\right) = x^3 - \\frac{1}{x} + x - \\frac{1}{x^3}.\n\\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\n4 \\left(18 + 1\\right) = 4 \\cdot 19 = 76.\n\\]\nSo:\n\\[\n67 = x^3 - \\frac{1}{x} + x - \\frac{1}{x^3}.\n\\]\nSince $x - \\frac{1}{x} = 4$, we have:\n\\[\nx^3 - \\frac{1}{x} + x - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 76,\n\\]\nand because $x - \\frac{1}{x} = 4$, we can solve for $x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3}$:\n\\[\nx^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 76 - 4 = \\boxed{72}.\n\\]",
